WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.940
This is an article published by the Guardian
back in 2014 ― It’s an obituary for Australia’s

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.599
Great Barrier Reef.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.180
Two years later, Outside magazine published this ― it’s...

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.380
another obituary for the reef.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.460
And more recently, we got this news:

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:20.180
BBC News: And also this hour, the Great Barrier Reef is at a terminal stage.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.759
Have we really killed the Great Barrier Reef?

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.359
The answer is no… but we sure are trying.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.560
It’d be hard to call the time of death for

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.120
the Great Barrier Reef because it’s actually some 3,000 reefs

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.660
spread over an area the size of Italy.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.280
So there’s plenty of room right now for
there to be widespread damage and lots of

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.190
relatively healthy reefs ― that’s something
dive operators there really want you to know.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:54.680
“The contrast in colors down there and the
water ― when the light hits the different

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.680
colors down there and yeah it’s absolutely
amazing.”

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.820
But the world’s coral reefs, including the
Great Barrier Reef, have had a really hard

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.490
time the past few years.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.670
After decades of degradation from local threats like pollution, and overfishing ―

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.880
coral reefs have now also undergone a record-breaking
“global bleaching event.”

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.940
That’s when coral turns white, which puts
them at a high risk of dying.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.390
It started in 2014, during the Northern Hemisphere
summer.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.960
Abnormally warm water caused corals in Guam
and the Northern Mariana Islands to start

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.360
bleaching en masse.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.940
By the end of 2014, corals around Hawaii,
Florida and the Marshall Islands

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.380
were bleaching too.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.460
When summer came around in the Southern Hemisphere, bleaching spreads to coral reefs

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.360
in the South Pacific and the Indian Ocean.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.909
And towards the end of 2015, corals throughout
the Caribbean were bleached too.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:43.110
Hawaii’s corals bleached for a second time.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.710
By now El Nino was in full force, and when
combined with global warming, it kept sea

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:53.120
temperatures high enough to continue the bleaching
event into 2016, hitting corals in Asia,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.100
the east coast of Africa, and ... the Great
Barrier Reef too

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.000
― the worst bleaching, in fact, that the
Great Barrier Reef had ever seen.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.200
Bleaching continued into 2017, when the Great
Barrier Reef was hit, again.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.460
ABC News: Australia’s Great Barrier Reef in grave
condition tonight...

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.200
FOX News: For the second year in a row, this
video showing bleaching…

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.240
BBC News: Two-thirds of the Great Barrier Reef
has now been devastated by severe coral bleaching

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.020
which is caused by rising water temperatures.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.920
Researchers first documented global coral
bleaching in 1998 ― a record warm year ―

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
and it happened again in 2010.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.760
But this third global bleaching event is by
far the worst.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.980
And looking at this temperature trend, you
can get why people are starting to say goodbye

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.580
to coral reefs.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.160
But to really get what makes them vulnerable,
you have to understand how coral reefs work.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.980
Corals are related to jellyfish and sea anemones,
but you’ve probably noticed that they don’t

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.040
look quite as... squishy.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.480
There are a couple reasons for that.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.670
For one thing, they live in colonies.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.180
Each coral structure is made of hundreds or
thousands of individual

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.500
coral animals called polyps.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.700
Each of these little bumps is where a polyp
lives.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.010
They’re easy to overlook if you’re snorkeling
but if you look closely, you can see them,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.820
especially at night when they’re less likely
to be hiding.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.460
Those polyps build a skeleton together.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:22.300
Not all corals do this but the ones that build
reefs do so by creating a calcium carbonate

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.650
skeleton underneath them, layer by layer.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.660
So the living polyps sit in little cups on
top of an ever-expanding skeleton structure

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:36.200
which, in turn, sits on top of the compacted
skeletons of previous corals from thousands

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.500
of years ago, otherwise known as limestone.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.080
By building these structures with all these
nooks and crannies, corals provide homes for

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.540
hundreds of other animals and plants ― an
estimated 25% of all marine species,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.820
even though they take up less than 1% of the ocean floor.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:57.060
Those reefs provide billions of dollars worth
of economic value to people every year, through

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.560
fisheries, tourism, and protection from storm
waves.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.060
But here’s the thing: corals can’t build
reefs on their own.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.560
Coral reefs exist because of an incredible
partnership between animal and plant.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.970
That’s because reef-building corals get
the majority of their energy and nutrients

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.760
from single-celled algae that live inside
coral polyps.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.420
It’s where they get their greenish-brown
color too.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.190
They’re called zooxanthellae, and like other
plants, they make energy from sunlight, that’s

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.210
why coral reefs mostly grow near the surface
of the ocean ― where the sun shines.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.930
But this partnership breaks down under heat
stress.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.650
After multiple weeks of temperatures even
just a couple degrees celsius hotter than

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:40.220
the maximum temperature that they’re used to,
The photosynthetic system in the algae starts

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.840
to accumulate reactive oxygen molecules like
hydrogen peroxide,

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.680
which leak into the coral polyp cells.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.160
To protect themselves from damage, the coral
polyps kick the algae out of their bodies,

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.560
leaving the pale skeleton showing through.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:00.310
By warming the planet, we are, among many
other things, breaking up the team that built

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:05.560
the ocean’s most diverse ecosystem.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.060
But bleached corals don’t necessarily die.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.850
What happens next depends on how severe and
long-lasting the high temperatures are.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.580
When researchers assessed the damage to the
Great Barrier Reef in 2016,

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.910
they found that coral death was concentrated
in the northern section of the reef, where

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.720
bleaching had been the most severe.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.520
In the central section, 33% of the reefs bleached
severely, but there was only around 6% mortality.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:33.380
That’s because zooxanthellae can return
to a coral colony within a few weeks if the

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.780
water cools back down fast enough.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.940
If not, the coral dies from starvation or
disease.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.830
If enough coral colonies die, a reef can get
taken over by fuzzy brown seaweed.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:49.100
Some coral reefs have transformed into…
this.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:53.169
If there are enough fish and other grazers
to eat up the seaweed, new coral larvae can

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:55.770
settle there and the reef can start building
up again.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:00.900
After the 1998 bleaching, the corals of the
Great Barrier Reef eventually recovered.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.550
But it takes a decade or more for even the
fastest growing corals to build back up, and

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.729
that 10 year timeline assumes one very important
condition:

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:11.400
That they don’t bleach all over again.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.740
And they almost certainly will.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:18.520
Climate models project that in the coming
decades, the conditions now causing mass bleaching

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.100
will become increasingly frequent, until eventually
they happen every summer.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.600
How soon that happens depends a lot on whether
we start cutting our greenhouse gas emissions.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.380
If we don’t, annual bleaching conditions
are projected for parts of

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.860
the Great Barrier Reef by mid-century.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.800
At that point, few coral reefs could survive.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.160
If we buy them more time by slowing down global
warming, corals and their zooxanthellae may

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:47.280
be able to acclimate or eventually evolve
to tolerate warmer weather.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.300
Coral reefs would still change, and probably
still shrink,

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.220
but we could give them a better chance.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.700
That’s why it doesn’t make sense to pronounce
them dead.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.400
This ecosystem is dynamic.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.320
There are forces building it up, that are  battling the forces tearing it down,

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.420
and those factors vary by species &amp; by location.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.620
But with global warming, humans have sided
against the world’s coral reefs

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.860
to an unprecedented degree.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.220
Some of the damage is now unavoidable.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:16.600
But the battle isn’t over, and it’s not
too late for us switch sides.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.040
